#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# eye_loopback.xdc — timing + IODELAY constraints for the eye_loopback target
# (DS-P2.66 Phase 5b). Pin LOCATIONS for led + jc are generated by
# `rr board pins-for-target` from routertl/boards/zybo-z7-20/pinout.yml
# (hardware.pins in targets/eye_loopback.yml) — NOT hand-listed here.
#
# This file constrains:
#   1. The PS7 fabric clocks (clk_fpga_0 / clk_fpga_1) async relationship.
#   2. The MMCM-derived 200 MHz IDELAYCTRL refclk + the swept eye TX clock.
#   3. The eye IODELAY_GROUP location/regclk reference.
#------------------------------------------------------------------------------

#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PS7 fabric clocks. clk_fpga_0 = 100 MHz (clk_sys), clk_fpga_1 = 125 MHz
# (clk_ddr — instantiated but parked in this target). The two PS7 clocks are
# asynchronous; bound the (unused-but-present) crossing with -datapath_only so
# STA never reports a bogus huge skew on the parked clk_ddr domain. The eye
# datapath lives entirely on the MMCM-derived clocks below, not on clk_fpga_1.
#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
set_max_delay -datapath_only 8.000 -from [get_clocks clk_fpga_0] -to [get_clocks clk_fpga_1]
set_max_delay -datapath_only 8.000 -from [get_clocks clk_fpga_1] -to [get_clocks clk_fpga_0]

#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MMCM-generated clocks.
#
# u_refclk_mmcm (MMCME2_ADV) derives 200 MHz on CLKOUT0 (×10 /5) from
# clk_fpga_0; Vivado auto-creates a generated clock on that MMCM output, but we
# name it explicitly for the IDELAYCTRL REFCLK_FREQUENCY=200 contract and so the
# 200 MHz domain is constrained even before place. The swept eye TX clock comes
# from u_eye_mmcm (clk_mmcm_xilinx) and is likewise auto-derived; its base
# profile is 100 MHz (CLKOUT0_DIVIDE=10), so 10 ns is the tightest period the
# eye datapath must meet at the base FREQ_SEL.
#
# Vivado propagates generated clocks through the MMCM automatically from the
# create_clock on the PS7 fclk; no explicit create_generated_clock is needed
# for functional correctness. We rely on the auto-derived names. If a future
# tightening is needed, name them via get_pins on the MMCM CLKOUT0 nets.
#------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The refclk-200 and eye-clk MMCM output domains are asynchronous to the PS7
# control clock for the few CDC arcs (EYE_CONTROL decode in clk_sys → eye_clk
# START/LD, eye counters eye_clk → clk_sys telemetry). The eye_engine + regbank
# telemetry crossings are slow-changing static control/status (read long after
# DONE), so treat clk_sys <-> eye_clk as an async group for those arcs.
# eye_clk and refclk200 share clk_fpga_0 as their MMCM source but run at
# unrelated phases/freqs → false-path the control/status crossings.
set_false_path -from [get_clocks clk_fpga_0] -to [get_pins -hier -filter {NAME =~ *u_eye/*START*}] -quiet
set_false_path -from [get_pins -hier -filter {NAME =~ *u_eye/*/ERROR_COUNT*}] -to [get_clocks clk_fpga_0] -quiet

#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IODELAY_GROUP "eye_iodelay" — the IDELAYE2 + IDELAYCTRL in idelay_scan(xilinx)
# both carry `attribute IODELAY_GROUP = "eye_iodelay"` so Vivado co-locates them
# in one clock region. The IDELAYCTRL REFCLK is the 200 MHz refclk200 above.
# No explicit set_property is required — the RTL attribute drives the grouping.
#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
